    "evidence_text": "Myosin Light Chain\u2013activating Phosphorylation Sites Are Required for Oogenesis in Drosophila \n\nGerm line cystoblasts entirely lacking a functional sqh gene show severe defects in proliferation and cytokinesis.\n\nIn addition, numerous aggregates of myosin heavy chain accumulate in the sqh null cells.\n\nFlies expressing sqh -A20, in which only the secondary phosphorylation site is absent, appear to be entirely wild type.\n\nThe Hippo signaling pathway restricts the oncogenic potential of an intestinal regeneration program.\n\nWe find that regenerating crypts express elevated Yes-associated protein (YAP) levels.\n\nConversely, hyperactivation of YAP results in widespread early-onset polyp formation following DSS treatment.\n\nA genetic screen identifies an LKB1\u2013MARK signalling axis controlling the Hippo\u2013YAP pathway\n\nThe Hippo\u2013YAP pathway is an emerging signalling cascade involved in the regulation of stem cell activity and organ size.\n\nTo identify components of this pathway, we performed an RNAi-based kinome screen in human cells.\n\nOur screen identified several kinases not previously associated with Hippo signalling that control multiple cellular processes.\n\nOur data also indicate that YAP is functionally important for the tumour suppressive effects of LKB1.\n\nIn addition, our findings provide insight into upstream signals of the Hippo\u2013YAP signalling cascade.\n\nRole of inscuteable in orienting asymmetric cell divisions in Drosophila\n\nMoreover, ectopic expression of inscuteable in other epithelial cells leads to spindle reorientation.\n\nPhosphate and R2D2 restrict the substrate specificity of Dicer-2, an ATP-driven ribonuclease.\n\nWhat makes the two Dicers specific for their biological substrates?\n\nMechanosignaling through YAP and TAZ drives fibroblast activation and fibrosis.\n\nHowever, the molecular mechanisms underlying this process remain poorly understood.\n\nTEAD mediates YAP-dependent gene induction and growth control.\n\nThe YAP transcription coactivator has been implicated as an oncogene and is amplified in human cancers.\n\nRecent studies have established that YAP is phosphorylated and inhibited by the Hippo tumor suppressor pathway.\n\nHere we demonstrate that the TEAD family transcription factors are essential in mediating YAP-dependent gene expression.\n\nCTGF is identified as a direct YAP target gene important for cell growth.\n\nA conserved phosphatase cascade that regulates nuclear membrane biogenesis.\n\nHerein, we demonstrate that human Dullard and the yeast protein Nem1p perform similar functions in mammalian cells and yeast cells, respectively.\n\nHowever, KRAS has thus far not been a viable therapeutic target.\n\nRegulation of Hippo pathway transcription factor TEAD by p38 MAPK-induced cytoplasmic translocation",
